{
  "step_1": "The decision reason is based on skewness, specifically preferring the lottery that is more right-skewed (i.e., assigns higher probability to unfavorable outcomes relative to favorable ones). For Lottery A: it yields -3000 Euros with 100% probability — this is a degenerate distribution with no variation, so it has no skewness in the usual sense, but can be considered maximally right-skewed in a deterministic sense since the unfavorable outcome (loss of 3000) is certain. For Lottery B: it yields -4000 with 80% probability and 0 with 20% probability. The majority of the probability (80%) is on the more negative outcome (-4000), which is unfavorable, while 20% yields the best possible outcome (0). Thus, Lottery B is right-skewed: high probability on a bad outcome, low probability on a good one. Comparing both: Lottery B has a clear right skew (80% on worst outcome), while Lottery A is certain at an intermediate loss. Since the reason prefers the lottery that is *more* right-skewed, it would prefer Lottery B over A. Therefore, the decision reason can be applied, and it would lead to choosing B.",
