  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on skewness—specifically preferring more right-skewed lotteries (those with higher probability on worse outcomes). In this case, B is more right-skewed than A, so the reason would select B. The individual also selects B, but for a different reason: lower expected loss. The information considered by the individual (outcomes, probabilities, expected value) overlaps partially with the inputs needed for skewness (which also uses outcomes and probabilities), but the processing differs. Skewness involves assessing the shape of the distribution, particularly where the bulk of the probability lies relative to outcomes. The individual instead performs a quantitative integration of outcomes and probabilities to compute expected loss. Their justification is purely in terms of magnitude of expected loss, not distributional asymmetry. Therefore, while both the reason and the individual arrive at B, the cognitive process described does not align with skewness evaluation. The verbal report shows no evidence of considering distribution shape or concentration of probability on unfavorable outcomes. Hence, it is unlikely the individual used the skewness-based reason.",
