  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on preferring the more right-skewed lottery, defined as assigning more probability to unfavorable outcomes. However, Lottery A is not clearly more right-skewed—it assigns 50% to the most favorable outcome (0€), which contradicts the definition. The individual does not discuss skewness, probability distributions, or the concentration of risk on bad outcomes. Instead, they focus on the positive aspect of a chance to avoid loss and the psychological mitigation of a larger loss due to context. Their reasoning aligns more with loss aversion, probability neglect, or the 'possibility effect' (valuing a chance to avoid loss highly) rather than skewness considerations. There is no evidence that the individual assessed the skew of the distributions or used such a rule to decide. The information considered (chance to lose nothing, perceived severity of losses) does not match the information required by the skewness reason (distribution of probabilities across favorable vs. unfavorable outcomes). The justification rule (desirability of zero loss, reduced pain of larger loss) is also different from the skewness-based preference rule. Therefore, it is unlikely the individual used the skewness reason.",
